# Cropping and straightening, with a preview that shows what is being cut.
#
# THE PREVIEW TOGGLE IS THE WHOLE INTERACTION. A crop that only ever shows the
# cropped result is nearly unusable while setting it: the part being cut away is
# off screen the moment a slider moves, so there is no way to judge where the
# edge should sit. Nothing is lost -- the input is still there -- but the viewer
# is not showing it.
#
#   preview on   the FULL frame, with the crop rectangle drawn and the outside
#                dimmed. Move the sliders and watch the rectangle.
#   preview off  the cropped raster, at its new size.
#
# Same parameters either way. Set the rectangle with preview on, then turn it
# off to apply.
#
# STRAIGHTENING IS PART OF THE SAME OPERATION, not a separate stage. Rotating a
# frame leaves empty wedges in the corners that have to be cropped away, and
# cropping first then rotating puts them back. Doing both at once means the
# rectangle is measured in the ROTATED frame, so the preview shows exactly what
# comes out -- corners included.
#
# The angle rotates about the CENTRE OF THE RECTANGLE rather than of the frame.
# About the frame centre the rectangle would swing away as the angle changed,
# and the two modes would show different parts of the image for the same
# settings.
#
# The trims are FRACTIONS of the frame, not pixels, so the same crop means the
# same thing on a preview-sized image and a full-sized one -- and on every frame
# of a group whatever their sizes.

src = image("test")

# check() rather than slider(): this is a toggle, and a 0..1 slider is a poor
# way to spell one. crop's own `preview` parameter is a Param<bool>, so it draws
# as a checkbox in the inspector too -- this only controls it from the script.
preview = check("preview the rectangle", 1)

left   = slider("trim left",   0, 0.9, 0)
right  = slider("trim right",  0, 0.9, 0)
top    = slider("trim top",    0, 0.9, 0)
bottom = slider("trim bottom", 0, 0.9, 0)

angle  = slider("straighten (degrees)", -20, 20, 0)

src => crop(preview = preview,
            left = left, right = right, top = top, bottom = bottom,
            angle = angle)
    => display("crop")

# A crop that trims nothing and rotates nothing is a no-op, and the pipeline
# aliases it away entirely -- so leaving this stage permanently in a chain costs
# nothing until it is actually used. (Only with preview OFF: a preview really
# does change the pixels, so it always runs.)
